Good call 👍 — when things get messy, a full clean reset is the fastest way to fix Kubernetes worker nodes.
Follow this exact order on EACH worker node 👇
STEP 1: Reset Kubernetes
sudo kubeadm reset -f
STEP 2: Stop services
sudo systemctl stop kubelet
sudo systemctl stop containerd
🧹 STEP 3: Remove Kubernetes + configs
sudo apt purge -y kubeadm kubectl kubelet kubernetes-cni
sudo apt autoremove -y
sudo rm -rf /etc/kubernetes/
sudo rm -rf /var/lib/kubelet/
sudo rm -rf /var/lib/etcd/
sudo rm -rf ~/.kube
🧹 STEP 4: Clean container runtime (IMPORTANT)
sudo rm -rf /etc/containerd
sudo rm -rf /var/lib/containerd
🧹 STEP 5: Clean networking leftovers
sudo iptables -F
sudo iptables -t nat -F
sudo iptables -t mangle -F
sudo iptables -X
sudo ip link delete cni0 || true
sudo ip link delete flannel.1 || true
🧹 STEP 6: Reboot node (RECOMMENDED)
sudo reboot

🚀 AFTER REBOOT — Fresh Install
Now reinstall cleanly 👇
🔧 1. Disable swap
sudo swapoff -a
sudo sed -i '/ swap / s/^/#/' /etc/fstab
🔧 2. Install containerd (clean)
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y containerd
sudo mkdir -p /etc/containerd
sudo containerd config default | sudo tee /etc/containerd/config.toml
Edit config:
sudo nano /etc/containerd/config.toml
👉 Set:
SystemdCgroup = true
🔧 3. Start containerd
sudo systemctl restart containerd
sudo systemctl enable containerd
🔧 4. Install Kubernetes
sudo mkdir -p /etc/apt/keyrings
curl -fsSL https://pkgs.k8s.io/core:/stable:/v1.29/deb/Release.key | \
sudo gpg --dearmor -o /etc/apt/keyrings/kubernetes.gpg
echo "deb [signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/kubernetes.gpg] \
https://pkgs.k8s.io/core:/stable:/v1.29/deb/ /" | \
sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/kubernetes.list
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y kubelet kubeadm kubectl
sudo apt-mark hold kubelet kubeadm kubectl

////Master Node Only
🔗 5. Join cluster (NEW TOKEN)

On master node:

kubeadm token create --print-join-command

Run output on worker:

sudo kubeadm join <MASTER_IP>:6443 --token <TOKEN> \
--discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:<HASH>
